{% set local_problem %}
<p>Athens' older neighborhoods face a drainage challenge that surface solutions can't fully address. Many homes in established areas near Athens State University, around downtown, and throughout central Limestone County were built with drainage systems designed for a different era. Those original systems have been patched, modified, and worked around for decades — but they've never been truly upgraded.</p>

<p>The result: downspouts that dump water too close to foundations, surface extensions that look ugly and get damaged by mowers, splash blocks that erode over time, and persistent moisture problems that never quite go away. Add Limestone County's clay-heavy soil that doesn't absorb water quickly, and you have foundations sitting in saturated soil for days after every heavy rain.</p>

<p>Surface solutions only move water a few feet from your foundation. On clay soil, that's not far enough — the water just pools and slowly migrates back toward your home. Underground drains solve this permanently by routing water 10, 20, or even 50+ feet away, far enough that it can never threaten your foundation.</p>

<p>Athens' rolling topography is actually an advantage here. Unlike flat subdivisions where underground drains have to fight gravity, Athens properties often have natural slopes that move water away efficiently. We design systems that work with your property's terrain, routing water downhill to natural discharge points.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Underground drains are buried pipes that connect to your downspouts and carry water through a sealed, sloped channel to a discharge point far from your home. The water exits at a predetermined location — typically at the property edge, into a storm drain (where permitted), or into a dry well that allows gradual absorption.</p>

<p>We use 4-inch corrugated or solid PVC pipe, depending on your specific application and soil conditions. The system is designed with proper slope (typically 1-2% grade) to ensure water flows away continuously rather than pooling inside the pipe. Pop-up emitters or daylight exits release water at the discharge point.</p>

<p>Unlike French drains (which collect groundwater that's already in the soil), underground gutter drains specifically handle roof runoff before it reaches the ground. They work in conjunction with your gutter system to create complete water management — from the moment rain hits your roof until it's safely away from your home.</p>
{% endset %}

